# 11. UNIFIED PROPERTY INVENTORY ARCHITECTURE

This section is critical because Great Homes will support internal listings and external Owner/Partner submissions.

We want one unified inventory architecture, not separate public listing engines.

Investigate the current data model and establish how this can be achieved safely.

## 11.1 Three listing sources

The future platform will support:

1. GREAT HOMES LISTED — created internally by authorized staff.
2. OWNER LISTED — submitted by a property owner.
3. PARTNER LISTED — submitted by a property agent/partner.

All three must ultimately be managed in the same Great Homes resale/rental inventory and public discovery systems.

Resale and Rental remain distinct listing types where their property fields and business rules differ.

## 11.2 Owner and Partner submission requirements

A future public submission portal will support:

- Customer mobile OTP login through the existing 2Factor integration.
- Property Owner account type.
- Listing Partner / Agent account type.
- Property submission.
- Photo submission.
- Asking price or rent.
- Property details.
- Submitter contact details.
- My Listings dashboard.
- Edit own submission.
- Track review and publication status.

The owner/partner must not be able to approve or publish their own listing.

Great Homes staff retain control over verification, approval, publication, unpublication, archive and restore.

## 11.3 Critical agent privacy rule

For an agent/partner submission:

- Store the agent's own verified contact details as the submitter/representative contact.
- Do not require the actual property owner's phone number or email.
- Do not expose the actual owner's private contact details to the partner portal.
- The partner must attest that they have obtained the owner's authorization to market and submit the property.
- This attestation must not be treated as independent proof of ownership or authorization.
- Great Homes may conduct its own verification process.

For an owner submission, the submitter's own contact details represent the owner contact.

## 11.4 Attribution and traceability

The system should distinguish:

- Listing source.
- Submitter account.
- Actual property owner, where known.
- Listing representative/agent, where applicable.
- Great Homes reviewer.
- Staff member who approved or published.
- Staff member who last edited the listing.

Proposed public attribution labels:

- Owner Listed.
- Partner Listed.
- Great Homes Listed.

Inspect whether the existing data model supports these distinctions.

## 11.5 Submission and listing relationship

Investigate whether an external submission can become an existing resale or rental listing without creating duplicate records.

The future architecture should preserve:

- Original submission ID.
- Final listing ID.
- Submitter identity.
- Source attribution.
- Submission history.
- Admin review history.
- Media relationships.
- All relevant changes.

An owner or partner editing their submission must not create a new unrelated property record each time.

Proposed submission workflow for investigation:

`DRAFT → SUBMITTED → UNDER_REVIEW → CHANGES_REQUESTED → APPROVED → PUBLISHED`

Additional withdrawal and archive handling should be considered.

The submitter must not directly control administrative lifecycle states.

## 11.6 Unified admin inventory

Investigate the feasibility of a common admin listing-management experience with:

- Resale and Rental filters.
- Great Homes Listed / Owner Listed / Partner Listed filters.
- Draft, Submitted, Under Review, Approved, Published, Paused and Archived filters.
- Review and verification workflow.
- Source attribution.
- Submission history.
- Listing history.
- Restore and relist actions.

Do not assume a complete single table or schema redesign is necessary. Recommend the safest architecture after inspecting the actual implementation.

